The rules of the Nine Chapters are also known as the Nine Admonitions. The content is as follows:

Heaven's great virtue bears the name of Life;
To wound or kill — this must not be done.
A man is measured by the strength of his word;
To break faith or betray a vow — this must not be done.
Let truth be manifest and falsehood driven out;
To deceive and mislead the many — this must not be done.

Govern the self with humility, bind conduct to modesty;
To indulge in arrogance and tyranny — this must not be done.
Honour the worthy, extend courtesy, and let order be the measure of propriety;
To act with wanton excess and unbridled abandon — this must not be done.
Let compassion and righteousness guide your mind and every deed;
To oppress the weak by force — this must not be done.

Let your heart be pure, your conduct restrained, and uprightness be the mark of integrity;
To bend the law for greed or cruelty — this must not be done.
Let clarity be your unwavering guide in discerning right from wrong;
To confuse truth and falsehood — this must not be done.
Attend to Heaven's edict with a reverent mind and a solemn heart;
To rebel against the sacred order — this must not be done.
